# EP04 — How to Survive the Wild Hunt (According to Folklore)
# id | picture | tokens | caption | narration      (ward lines: id | picture | ward ... | NUMERAL | rule | narration)
# [S1] Jacob Grimm, "Deutsche Mythologie" (1835; English: "Teutonic Mythology", trans. J. S. Stallybrass, 1882-88), ch. 31: the "Wütendes Heer" (furious host) and the Wild Hunt, linked to Wodan. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Hunt
# [S2] D. L. Ashliman, "Wild Huntsman Legends" (University of Pittsburgh), translations of: Grohmann, "Sagen-Buch von Böhmen und Mähren" (1863), pp. 78-80 (lie face down; the tradesman who looked up; the hind quarter of a horse thrown through the window of anyone who calls back); Thorpe, "Northern Mythology" vol. 3 (1852) ("The Wild Huntsman's Present": the hind quarter of an ox at the drunkard's door; "The Eternal Huntsman of Wynendael"); Haas, "Rügensche Sagen und Märchen" (1903) (a woman's leg in a red shoe); Colshorn, "Märchen und Sagen aus Hannover" (1854) (the huntsman spares those in the middle of the path). https://sites.pitt.edu/~dash/huntsman.html
# [S3] Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Peterborough manuscript (E), entry for 1127: black huntsmen on black horses and black goats, black wide-eyed hounds, seen from the arrival of Abbot Henry of Poitou through Lent to Easter, from Peterborough to Stamford. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterborough_Chronicle ; History Today, "The Wild Hunt in England" https://www.historytoday.com/archive/out-margins/wild-hunt-england
# [S4] Orderic Vitalis, "Ecclesiastical History", book 8: the priest Walchelin of Bonneval sees the "familia Herlechini" on the night of 1 January 1091, tries to seize a riderless horse as proof, is burned and seized by the throat. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Hunt
# [S5] Walter Map, "De nugis curialium" (c. 1180s): King Herla, who stayed three days at a fairy wedding, came back centuries later, and rides on forever. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Herla
# [S6] Austria.info (Austrian National Tourist Office), "A Raucous Christmas: Winter Traditions in Austria", and Stuttgarter Zeitung, "Aberglaube: Warum soll man zwischen den Jahren keine Wäsche waschen?": the Rauhnächte (Twelve Nights) and the custom of not hanging laundry out. https://www.austria.info/en-us/inspiration/raucous-traditions ; https://www.stuttgarter-zeitung.de/wissen/aberglaube-warum-soll-man-zwischen-den-jahren-keine-waesche-waschen-79207640.html
# [S7] Wikipedia, "Gabriel Hounds": the northern English "Gabriel ratchets", explained as the night calls of migrating wild geese. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_Hounds
# [S8] Wikipedia, "Harlequin": the name likely comes from "Hellequin", a demon leader linked to the Wild Hunt in medieval French tradition. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlequin
# [S9] William Shakespeare, "The Merry Wives of Windsor" (c. 1597), Act 4 Scene 4: Herne the Hunter, who "walks round about an oak" at midnight in winter.

# ---- HOOK ----
h01 | a lonely farm road at night between bare trees and a small farmhouse with one lit window, wide scene | wind night zoomin | Christmas week | It's the week after Christmas, a long time ago, and you're walking home late. The village is still a mile away. And the wind has just changed.
h02 | a farmer in a long cloak and a wide brimmed hat walking along a road holding a lantern, full figure | walk wind night | | First, the dogs in the farmyards go quiet. Then, high above you, you hear them. Hounds. Dozens of hounds, baying in the sky. Horns. Hooves on nothing. And something like laughter in the wind.
h03 | a ghostly procession of skeletal horsemen and black hounds flying through stormy clouds high in the night sky, wide scene | panr wind night | | Look up, and you might see them. Riders on black horses, crossing the clouds. Some of them are dead. Some of them are people you used to know. And the one at the front, the hunter, is looking for company.
h04 | =h02 | shiver night | | All over Europe, from Norway to the Alps to the English fens, people told stories about this. They called it the furious host. The ghostly ride. The Wild Hunt.
h05 | - | quote | They kill anyone they meet who does not throw himself down with his face to the ground. — a Bohemian legend, 1863 | And almost everywhere, they told you the same thing. If it catches you on the road, you have seconds to decide what to do.
h06 | an open old leather-bound book with blank pages and a melting candle beside it | title wind | the Wild Hunt | This is the Ward Book. The last chapter of the year. And tonight, how to survive the Wild Hunt.

# ---- WHO RIDES ----
h07 | a one-eyed old god with a long beard and a wide hat riding an eight-legged horse through storm clouds, full figure | breathe wind | Wodan? | Who's leading it depends on where you live. In Germany and Scandinavia, it was often Wodan, or Odin. The one-eyed god, galloping through the storm with the souls of the dead behind him.
h08 | an old scholar with long hair and a high collar writing in a big book by candlelight, full figure | breathe dust | Jacob Grimm, 1835 | That link was made famous by Jacob Grimm. Yes, the fairy tale Grimm. In eighteen thirty-five, in his huge book on German mythology, he gathered the legends of the furious host and argued they were a memory of the old god's ride.
h09 | a medieval king in a crown riding a horse with a small dog sitting in front of him on the saddle, full figure | breathe | King Herla | But the riders change from country to country. In England, a twelfth-century writer named Walter Map told of King Herla, who spent three days at a fairy wedding, came home to find two hundred years had passed, and has been riding ever since.
h10 | a hunter wearing stag antlers on his head standing beside a huge old oak tree in moonlight, full figure | sway night | Herne the Hunter | In Windsor, it was Herne the Hunter, the antlered ghost that Shakespeare put into The Merry Wives of Windsor, walking round an oak at midnight in the dead of winter.
h11 | a cursed nobleman in hunting clothes blowing a horn, with hounds at his feet, full figure | breathe wind | Hunt forever | And in a Dutch legend, a lord who loved hunting more than anything is cursed by his own dying father. Hunt then forever, the old man cries. Aye, forever. And so he does.

# ---- WARD I ----
h12 | a crescent moon above a snowy roof with twelve small stars in a row in the sky | ward snow night | I | Know the Twelve Nights | Ward one. Know the Twelve Nights.
h13 | =h12 | zoomin snow night | The Rauhnächte | The Hunt can ride on any wild night, especially in autumn and winter. But in the German-speaking Alps, one stretch of the year is its favourite. The Rauhnächte. The twelve nights between Christmas and Epiphany.
h14 | a family sitting together at a table in a small wooden room with a candle, the shutters closed, wide scene | breathe dust | | It's the time between the years. The old year is dead, and the new one hasn't properly started. In that gap, the stories say, the door between worlds hangs open. So be home before dark, close the shutters, and keep the candles lit.

# ---- WARD II ----
h15 | a drunk peasant with a mug of ale shouting up at the sky with his hand cupped to his mouth, full figure | ward wag | II | Never call out to the Hunt | Ward two. Never, ever call out to the Hunt.
h16 | =h15 | zoomin | | This is the most common warning in the whole legend. And the stories are honestly a little bit funny, in a horrible way. In a Dutch tale, a drunk man hears the hunt pass over, and he shouts up, Hey, Sir Hunter! Give me part of your game!
h17 | the hind leg of a cow hanging from a hook on a wooden farmhouse door, close up | sway night | | The next morning, his wife opens the front door, and finds the hind quarter of an ox hanging there. He asked for a share. He got one.
h18 | =h17 | zoomin night | | And it gets worse. In a Bohemian telling, anyone who calls back finds the back half of a horse thrown through their window. On the island of Rügen, in Germany, a worker who hollered along with the hunt had a woman's leg thrown at him. A leg still wearing a red shoe.
h19 | =h15 | breathe | | The lesson is very clear. The Hunt is not a parade. Don't cheer. Don't heckle. Don't ask for souvenirs. Whatever it's hunting, you do not want a slice of it.

# ---- WARD III ----
h20 | a peasant lying flat face down on a dirt road with his arms over his head, full figure | ward | III | Face down, and don't look up | Ward three. Throw yourself face down, and don't look up.
h21 | =h20 | zoomin | | This one comes straight from the legends of Bohemia, recorded in eighteen sixty-three. A peasant on a forest road hears the hunt coming. He throws himself down with his face to the ground, and the whole procession passes him by. Not a scratch.
h22 | a man kneeling on a road clutching his head in madness, eyes wide, full figure | shiver night | | A tradesman on the same road does the same thing. But he gets curious. He lifts his head, just for a moment, to watch them go by. And he goes mad.
h23 | =h20 | hold | | Another telling from the north of Germany adds one more detail. The huntsman spares those who stay in the very middle of the path. So, middle of the road, face in the dirt, eyes shut. It's undignified. It's also how you get home.

# ---- WARD IV ----
h24 | a riderless black horse with an ornate saddle standing in the dark, its stirrups glowing hot, full figure | ward night | IV | Take nothing from the Hunt | Ward four. Take nothing from the Hunt.
h25 | a young priest in a hooded robe standing on a road at night staring in fear, full figure | shiver night | Normandy, 1091 | On the night of the first of January, ten ninety-one, a young priest named Walchelin was walking home in Normandy, when a huge army of the dead passed him on the road. Peasants, women, monks, knights. Some of them he recognised. Some had died not long before.
h26 | =h24 | zoomin night | | Walchelin had a very modern thought. Nobody is going to believe me. I need proof. So he grabbed the reins of a horse with no rider.
h27 | =h25 | night | Orderic Vitalis | The stirrup burned his foot. The reins froze his hand. And a dead knight grabbed him by the throat, and left a mark that the monk Orderic Vitalis said he carried for the rest of his life. Orderic wrote the story down, and claimed he'd heard it from Walchelin himself.
h28 | =h24 | pulse | | So, if the Hunt drops something on the road, a horse, a horn, a coin, leave it. It's not lost. It's bait.

# ---- WARD V ----
h29 | a white bedsheet on a washing line between two posts flapping in the wind at night | ward sway wind night | V | Take the washing in | And the last ward. Take the washing in.
h30 | =h29 | zoomin wind night | | This one is still practised, half as a joke and half not, in parts of Germany and Austria today. During the Twelve Nights, don't hang your laundry outside.
h31 | =h03 | wind night panr | | Why? The explanations vary. Some say the riders get tangled in it, and you don't want them stuck in your garden. Others say a sheet the Hunt rides through becomes a shroud. And whoever sleeps in it will be dead before the year is out.
h32 | =h14 | dust | | So, if your grandmother in Bavaria refuses to do the washing between Christmas and New Year, now you know. She isn't being lazy. She's being careful.

# ---- THE HUNT IN THE RECORDS ----
h33 | a medieval stone abbey church with a tall tower and a deer park beside it, wide scene | panl | Peterborough, 1127 | And here's the part that gives me chills. The Wild Hunt shows up in a real English historical record. The Peterborough Chronicle, a version of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, kept by monks.
h34 | a pack of huge black hounds with wide staring eyes running, full figures | creep night | | In the entry for eleven twenty-seven, the monk writes that many men saw and heard huntsmen hunting. They were black, and big, and loathsome. Their hounds were black, with wide staring eyes. And they rode on black horses, and black goats.
h35 | a greedy medieval abbot in rich robes holding a bag of coins, full figure | breathe | Abbot Henry | They were seen, he says, in the deer park of Peterborough and in the woods all the way to Stamford, and the monks heard horns blowing at night. It began right when a new abbot arrived. A man named Henry of Poitou, whom the monks absolutely hated.
h36 | =h35 | zoomin | | Was it a real sighting? A storm? Or a furious monk, using the oldest ghost story in Europe to say exactly what he thought of his new boss? Historians lean towards the last one. But it's still the earliest Wild Hunt in English writing.

# ---- WHAT WAS REALLY UP THERE ----
h37 | a skein of wild geese flying in a V across a full moon at night, wide scene | wind night panr | Gabriel's hounds | So what were people actually hearing? Part of the answer may be flying overhead. In the north of England, the baying in the sky had its own name, Gabriel's hounds. And many people explained it as wild geese, migrating at night, honking in the dark like a pack of dogs.
h38 | =h01 | wind night zoomout | | Add winter storms howling through the forest, a world with no street lights, and a deep Christian fear of restless souls of the dead. And you have the perfect conditions for an army in the clouds.
h39 | a grinning harlequin jester in a diamond patterned costume and a black mask, full figure | wag | Hellequin → Harlequin | And here's the twist I promised. In medieval France, one leader of the hunt was a demon called Hellequin. Over the centuries, his name drifted onto the stage, into Italian comedy, and he slowly became Harlequin. The clown in the diamond suit.
h40 | =h39 | zoomin | | So the king of the dead, who galloped across the sky collecting souls, ended up as a jester. Which is, honestly, the most folklore thing that has ever happened.

# ---- THE VERDICT ----
h41 | =h20 | breathe | | So, how do you survive the Wild Hunt? Know the Twelve Nights. Never call out. Face down in the middle of the road, and don't look up. Take nothing it leaves behind. And bring in the washing.
h42 | =h14 | zoomin dust | | Those wards kept people indoors, together, quiet and careful, on the darkest, coldest nights of the year. Which, if you think about it, was very good advice, ghosts or no ghosts.
h43 | =h06 | dust zoomin | | That's the end of the first chapter of the Ward Book. Four winter monsters, twenty wards. Krampus, the Wendigo, Baba Yaga, and the Wild Hunt. Tell me in the comments which legend we should open next, and the most-liked answer gets the next chapter.
h44 | =h03 | endcard wind night | Your choice | Subscribe so the Ward Book is open when the next one comes. And until then, if you hear hounds in the sky, you know what to do. Face down. Eyes shut. Happy Twelve Nights.
